  "notes": "R&B-pop alternating tropical/dance-house with sparse trap-R&B, encoded on the straight class the Lane line names. The trop-house grammar is stated in full and authored literally: \"kick on all fours, clap on 2&4, offbeat 8th shaker and a pitched pluck standing in for the hat\", so the kick anchors all four beats at near-zero syncopation, the perc lanes are `shaker` and `perc` at `offbeat` placement, and `hihat.base` is 8th with the roll vocabulary held very low because the hat is a pluck rather than a hat. \"The R&B mode uses a half-time kick/snare with finger snaps and very sparse hats\" survives as the `snap` lane and the low `fillDensity`. The 808 is authored because the entry names it — \"808 sub with short slides in the R&B mode; a filtered pumping synth sub in the dance mode\" — as `counter_riff`, and \"low end drops out entirely under pre-choruses\" is the deep arrangement drop-out. Harmony is \"4-bar minor with 7ths\". The melody block carries the documented topline: \"melisma-light, syllabic, and written around a repeated pivot note\", which is the high step share, the tight `motifPitchCount` and the low `octaveJumpProb`; the Bellion-era signature, \"stacked choir-like vocal beds\", is the `sustain_pad` counter at a high density. The arrangement encodes the entry's structural claim that the drop is instrumental rather than vocal, with the pitched-pluck post-chorus alternating with the sung chorus. ⚠ `confidence` is medium: the research rates it medium-high and says \"tempo/key bands lane-derived\". Every drum probability, the humanize numbers, the key/scale weighting and `bpm.mode` 97 (the midpoint of the first-named 90—105 trop-house band) are INFERRED, and the entry's [LOW-CONF] mark on the falsetto-default claim is repeated here.",
